  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n    We address the Erdos minimum overlap problem by proposing a multi-resolution optimization scheme with structured refinement. The core idea is to:\n    1. Initialize using provided parent solution\n    2. Iterate between coarse and fine resolutions\n    3. At each resolution, perform intensive local optimization with adaptive penalty\n    4. Refine solution by projection and polishing\n    5. Terminate when time exceeds budget\n\n    Key improvements over current approach:\n    - Dynamic resolution adjustment without grid interpolation\n    - Penalty parameter scaling with problem size\n    - More aggressive gradient projection for faster convergence\n    - Improved expense tracking with explicit time-outs\n    - Structured multi-phase optimization with increasing intensity\n\n    The algorithm is robust to multimodality by:\n    \u2022 Using multiple restarts per resolution\n    \u2022 Combining exact gradients with subgradient polishing\n    \u2022 Maintaining the best solution across all resolutions\n\n    Implementation steps:\n\n    1. Initialization Setup:\n- Set default parent solution if available\n- Establish resolution progression list\n- Initialize timing and best solution storage\n\n    2. Create_Helper_Functions:\n    \u2022 Custom optimizer using SLSQP with projected boundaries\n    \u2022 Subgradient polishing with adaptive step sizes\n    \u2022 Feasibility projection maintaining integral constraint\n    \u2022 Function to compute correlation integral\n    \u2022 Time-aware optimization control\n\n    3. Multi-phase Optimization:\n    Phase1 (Intensity warmup):\n- Start from minimal resolution \n- Gradually increase resolution while optimizing\n- Dynamically adjust penalty parameter\n\n    Phase2 (Deep refinement):\n- For each active resolution:\n    \u2022 6 restarts from projected neighborhood\n    \u2022 Intensive SLSQP with increased iterations\n    \u2022 Adaptive polishing sequence\n- Update best solution based on final objectives\n\n    Phase3 (Time-controlled polish):\n- Before final deadline:\n    \u2022 Execute one final refinement step\n    \u2022 Use most aggressive gradient-based method\n    \u2022 Fall back to random restart if improvement stalls\n\n    4. Resolution Management:\n    \u2022 Maintain feasible solution throughout\n    \u2022 Store best correlation across resolutions\n    \u2022 Use logarithmic scaling for penalty parameters\n    \u2022 Preserve integral constraint via post-processing\n\n    5. Termination:\n    \u2022 Return best solution found with its parameterization\n    \u2022 Verify feasibility of returned solution\n    \u2022 Ensure dimensionality preservation when scaling\n\n    6. Search Choices:\n    \u2022 Secondary optimization methods:\n        - Grid search projection\n        - Multistart around extrema\n        - Frequency domain adjustment\n    \u2022 Resolution: 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512\n    \u2022 Penalty factor: log-growth from 10^2 to 10^6\n\n    Compute budget:\n    \u2022 Expected runtime dominated by quadratic updates\n    \u2022 Must complete all but final phase within (budget_s-60) seconds\n    \u2022 Each resolution requires:\n        - 8 seconds per restart\n        - 100 function evaluations\n        - Adaptive termination\n\n    Implementation details:\n\n    1. Representation: Function is discretized with N points using project_sum to maintain \u222bh=1 and h\u2208[0,1]. When changing N, interpolate from closest maintained solution.\n\n    2. Projection and Constraint Handling:\n        \u2022 define function project_sum(h, target) that:\n            - Clips values to [0,1]\n            - Adjusts sums iteratively by binary search\n        \u2022 This maintains mathematical 0\u2264h\u22641 and \u222bh\u2248target\n\n    3. Optimizer Setup:\n        \u2022 Use SLSQP method with explicit constraints\n        \u2022 Objective: obj_grad(h, beta)\n        \u2022 Constraints: sum(h)\u2248target\n        \u2022 Bounds: [0,1] for each h[i]\n\n    4. Gradient Evaluation:\n        \u2022 Based on continuous version of correlation integral\n        \u2022 Handles all lags uniformly\n        \u2022 Includes slack variables to control strictness\n\n    5. Subgradient Polishing:\n        \u2022 Implements combined gradient + coordinate descent\n        \u2022 Uses adaptive step sizes: [0.1, 0.05, 0.025, 0.01, 0.005]\n        \u2022 Monitors improvement rate to adapt penalty\n\n    6. Time Handling:\n        \u2022 Compute elapsed time fraction\n        \u2022 Reduce intensity for edges seconds conservatively\n        \u2022 Prioritize higher resolutions when time remains\n\n    Validation and quality checks:\n        \u2022 Ensure \u222bh\u2248target by projection\n        \u2022 Check min(\u2207obj) < 1e-5 for termination\n        \u2022 Verify ergodic average improvement\n        \u2022 Maintain at least one feasible solution throughout\n\n    Final return contract:\n        \u2022 Return best solution with its length\n        \u2022 Project to nearest resolution if necessary\n        \u2022 Ensure direct compatibility with evaluator\n        \u2022 Handle dimension changes gracefully\n\n    Implementation structure:\n\n    def run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n        # (Code implementation follows...)",
